%erg 0.1
Title: The stall probe loses its whole run if it is stopped
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-16T11:08Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context

`bench/sdt_stall_probe.py` writes its result exactly once, after the sampling
loop has returned:

```python
if args.json_out:
    args.json_out.write_text(text + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    print(f"wrote {args.json_out}")
```

There is no `signal` handler anywhere in the module, and no incremental write.
A `SIGINT` or `SIGTERM` therefore destroys the entire run — every sample taken
so far, not merely the remainder.

The loop itself is well behaved and must be preserved: it stops when the
deadline passes **and** when the target dies (`except ProbeError: break`), which
is what let the 2026-09-16 AR6 run write its JSON at all. The defect is only in
what happens when the *operator* stops it.

**No data was actually lost on 2026-09-16.** The probe exited on its own when
Zotero quit. What the defect cost was different and worth naming precisely: the
probe had been launched with `--seconds 2400` against a window that turned out
to be mis-aimed — sampling began twenty seconds after the document settled — and
it could not be stopped and relaunched, because stopping it would have thrown
away the samples already taken. An instrument that cannot be interrupted cannot
be re-aimed, so a long run started on a wrong guess has to be waited out. That
is the cost, and it is recurring rather than hypothetical: these runs are
launched against live author activity that cannot be replayed.

## Relevant files

- `bench/sdt_stall_probe.py` — `main()` holds the single write; `sample()` (the
  `while True` loop with the `ProbeError` break) holds the interruption point.
- `verification/incidents/0793-2026-09-16-ar6-completed-run.md` — the run that
  exposed it, and the reason the window matters.

## Actions

1. Install handlers for `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` that request a stop rather than
   raising: set a flag the sampling loop checks alongside its deadline, so the
   loop returns normally and `main()` falls through to its existing summary and
   write path unchanged.
2. Keep both existing exits intact — deadline reached, and target died. The
   new stop is a third reason to leave the loop, not a replacement.
3. Report the stop reason in the JSON (`_run.stoppedBy`: `deadline`,
   `processGone`, `signal`) so a partial run is never mistaken for a complete
   one by a later reader. A truncated window that does not say it was truncated
   is the same class of defect as an all-clear indistinguishable from "I could
   not look".
4. Leave a second signal unhandled: if the operator insists, the default
   disposition should still kill it. Do not trap so thoroughly that the process
   becomes unkillable without `SIGKILL`.

## Test

Red first, and the control is the point. Launch the probe against a
deliberately long-lived target (`sleep 300`) with `--json-out`, wait for at
least two intervals, send `SIGTERM`, then assert:

- the JSON file exists,
- it parses,
- it contains the samples taken before the signal,
- `_run.stoppedBy == "signal"`.

Run that test against the **unfixed** module first and watch it fail on the
first assertion — the file must be absent. A test that passes before the fix
proves nothing about it.

## Verification

- [ ] `grep -n 'signal' bench/sdt_stall_probe.py` returns the handler
      installation (it returns nothing today — that is the positive control for
      this box).
- [ ] The new test fails on the pre-fix module at the "file exists" assertion,
      and passes after.
- [ ] A normal completed run still reports `_run.stoppedBy == "deadline"`, and a
      run whose target dies still reports `processGone`.
- [ ] `make check-fast` passes.

## Invariants

- The sampling loop's existing exits — deadline, and `ProbeError` when the
  target dies — keep working unchanged.
- The summary and band logic (`slowCpuPct`, `intervalsAboveSlow`) is untouched;
  this ticket moves no threshold and changes no verdict.
- A partial run must be distinguishable from a complete one by reading the JSON
  alone, with no reference to how it was launched.

## Exit criteria

- The probe, stopped by a signal after at least two intervals, leaves a valid
  JSON holding the samples it took and saying it was cut short.
- The red test above is committed, and its pre-fix failure is recorded in the
  PR body rather than asserted.
